          "derivation": "Pokorny cites the Persian word for this root by name: under reug- he writes 'npers. ā-rōγ ,das Rülpsen^'. آروغ āroγ 'belch' is the prefixed form, Old Iranian *ā- 'onto, up' + *raugah- (a neuter -as-stem from *h₁rewg-, cf. Greek ἐρυγή 'belch', Latin ē-rūgō), giving Middle/New Persian -rōγ with the regular Iranian *g > γ between vowels; the unprefixed رُغ/روغ roγ is the same *raugah- without ā-, and Mo'in lists the set together: 'آروغ [= آرغ = رُغ = وروغ] (اِ.) باد گلو', i.e. the variants آرغ, رُغ, وروغ (the last with a prothetic v-). آروق with qāf is a late orthographic variant (the -q spelling being the Turkic-influenced way of writing final -γ), listed as a variant by both Mo'in and Wiktionary. Cognates: Armenian որկամ, Greek ἐρεύγομαι, Latin ērūgō, Lithuanian rjáugmi, OCS rygati — an onomatopoeic root, so the Persian form is inherited rather than borrowed.",
