          "derivation": "The etymology is right, the citation form is slightly off. The Germanic strong verb is normally reconstructed as Proto-Germanic *reukaną 'to smoke, steam' (Watkins: 'Germanic *reu-kan'; Etymonline: 'from Proto-Germanic *reukan'), whence Old English rēocan (West Saxon) / recan (Anglian), Old High German riohhan, Middle Dutch rieken, Old Norse rjúka. Nourai's 'riukan' with -iu- reproduces the Old Norse/Old High German shape (rjúka, riohhan) rather than the Proto-Germanic one; Skeat similarly cites 'Icel. rjúka (pt. t. rauk), G. riechen' alongside A.S. rēocan. The sense given ('to produce vapor, smell, stink') is the Germanic branch of Pokorny's tentative 'Wolke, Rauch' extension of *reug-, and Pokorny marks that extension 'very doubtful' ('yet very doubtful') — so the link of the smoke-words to the belch-root is accepted but not certain.",
